\section{Separate Ownership from Placement}

An owner has authority to arrange destruction; an arena determines
where allocations are placed and when their storage is reclaimed.
The two concepts need not coincide. A unique vector owner can drop
its buffer individually, while a parse-job owner can drop thousands
of nodes at once by releasing an arena. A borrowed view grants
access without granting either authority. Rust's ownership rules
illustrate this distinction: borrowing a vector is not moving its
owner and does not make the borrower responsible for dropping it.
A language with region
inference goes further: it can choose placements and insert region
entry and exit using information about where values are stored and
which regions a computation accesses. Nagata, Kobayashi, and
Yonezawa investigate combining such inference with soft typing
for a dynamically typed language.

Consider a compiler server with three lifetimes. The process
owns an identifier interner. A compilation unit owns the retained
syntax tree. A single parse attempt owns a scratch arena holding
tokens and speculative nodes. A scratch node can refer to a unit
node and to an interned identifier. A unit node must not retain a
reference to scratch once the attempt ends. The boundary condition
is about accesses, not about whether the bytes have been cleared.
Reusing the very same scratch pages on a retry makes an invalid
reference particularly hard to diagnose because its address may
still point to an apparently valid node.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Holder & Target & Safe policy \\
\hline
Scratch node & Unit node & Borrow until scratch closes \\
Retained unit node & Scratch node & Copy or reject escape \\
Unit node & Interned name & Use process-stable ID \\
Interner & Unit-owned text & Copy bytes into process storage \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\Cref{fig:visual-409} depicts permitted stored references under a
simple outlives rule. A restricted language can enforce the rule on
field types; an inference pass can instead generate constraints on
placements. The order goes from a shorter-lived holder toward a
longer-lived target. A temporary read by outer code while the inner
scope is open is another matter: it can be valid even though saving
that temporary in an outer object would be invalid.

