% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Promote a Graph Before Closing the Attempt}

\begin{verbatim}
retain(node, scratch, unit, copied):
    if node is null: return null
    if owner(node) is not scratch:
        assert owner(node) outlives unit
        return node
    if copied contains node: return copied[node]
    result := unit.allocate_node(node.kind)
    copied[node] := result
    result.name := stable_name_id(node.name)
    for field in node.reference_fields:
        result[field] := retain(node[field], scratch, unit, copied)
    return result

parse_unit(tokens, unit):
    with scratch_attempt() as scratch:
        temporary := parse(tokens, scratch)
        validate(temporary)
        root := retain(temporary, scratch, unit, empty_map())
        return root
\end{verbatim}

Insert the mapping before visiting children so that shared nodes
remain shared and cycles terminate. Store only process-stable IDs
in the name field; a slice into token text would require its own
copy. The example assumes these are parse nodes without external
identity observers. If observers already hold references into
scratch, copying does not transfer those references. It creates
new objects. Promotion by copying is therefore much easier before
publishing the parse result. For a deeply nested tree, replace
recursive calls with a worklist; the lifetime proof does not rely
on the host call stack.

Now consider an error halfway through promotion. Scratch is still
closed by scope cleanup, but a monotonic unit arena may retain the
partly copied nodes. If a server retries often, build the retained
graph in a provisional arena owned by the unit and adopt that arena
only on success. A rewind marker in the unit arena can work
instead when no other allocation can intervene and no observers
have seen any object past the marker. In either implementation,
publication and successful transfer of ownership must be ordered:
the unit should never advertise a pointer to an arena the failure
path still intends to discard.

\section{Analyze Outlives Constraints at Closures and Loops}

If a function returns a closure capturing a temporary pair, the
pair must live as long as the closure, not just until the function
returns. One solution allocates the pair in a result region supplied
by the caller. Another extracts just the captured fields and places
them in the closure. The first preserves sharing with another
escaping reference to the pair; the second requires proof that
changing its representation preserves observable behavior. A
region-polymorphic helper can construct the same representation
in different destinations without baking a particular allocation
policy into the helper. Calls through unknown functions may require
conservative placement unless their interfaces state their effects.

Even correct placement can waste memory. Suppose each iteration
creates 4 KiB of temporary data and leaves only a 32-byte summary.
Putting both in a loop-wide arena retains roughly 4 GiB of temporary
data after a million iterations, even if no iteration keeps a
reference to its predecessors. Use an iteration arena for descent
and allocate the summary into a longer-lived destination before
reset. If an update to a retained summary starts storing a pointer
to the temporary, the transformation is no longer safe. Verify
effects of mutation as well as allocation sites.

\section{Make Storage Release and Cleanup Distinct}

Releasing an arena need not run a destructor for every object.
That is a feature for compiler nodes containing only bytes and
references, but wrong for objects owning files, locks, or foreign
resources. Restrict arena contents to trivially droppable objects,
manage resource owners outside the arena, or keep an explicit
cleanup log executed before releasing arena storage. Cleanup order
must fit the dependencies of those callbacks. If callbacks inspect
other arena objects, running them after returning the pages is not
an option; if callbacks can allocate, define whether the closing
arena rejects reentry.

For integration tests, run both the successful and failed parse
through the same small scratch pages so a retry immediately
overwrites freed data. Verify retained sharing, inspect the
live-byte curve over many retries, and separately test cleanup
on exceptions. If process-lifetime interned names keep growing
under rejected input, that is not a violation of the outlives
rule. It is a policy problem. A lifetime discipline proves that
a reference remains valid; it does not prove that the selected
owner releases storage at a useful time.

\section{Partition Parallel Work by Ownership}

The parser can also send subtrees to parallel diagnostic workers.
Giving each worker its own scratch arena avoids allocator locking,
but it does not allow the worker to retain a pointer into the
parent's scratch arena after the parent returns. A join inside
the attempt is one valid boundary: the attempt cannot close
until every child returns. A detached job instead needs a
snapshot in storage that the job owns, or a shared owner whose
lifetime extends through the job. Moving an arena handle can be
cheaper than copying the nodes when the whole arena transfers
and no outstanding borrows remain. It is not enough to copy the
handle if closing either copy would release the same storage.

For example, a job might return a diagnostic consisting of a
message, a file identifier, and two byte offsets. If its line
snippet is represented as a view into a temporary decoding
buffer, recycling that buffer invalidates the diagnostic even
though the byte offsets and message are safe. Either store an
owned snippet or reload it from an immutable source snapshot
whose version is part of the diagnostic. This is a useful review
question for every arena-backed API: among its returned fields,
which are owning values, which are identifiers, and which are
views with an expiration point? Grouping them into a single
record should not erase the answer.

Inspect memory after closing the last owner as well as after
each attempt. A steady process heap during compilation does
not distinguish successful reuse from storage mistakenly
retained across all units.
